      "dom_id": "question-103444",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-1,2)$ and $(4,3).$",
      "worked_solution": "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-1,2)$ and $(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-5.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(1,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-5x+5 \\\\ y & =-5x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
